nd out pretty quickly  when some of them have gotten into  serious  legal trouble in almost all of the cases  that we've documented to date after an  individual gets jailed on you know on  suspicion of whatever crime the Saudi  Arabian government as a policy and  they've told us this explicitly as a  policy will post the bail money for any  individual any citizen of theirs accused  of a crime in the United States and then  also provide money for legal  representation so they don't have a you  know a public defender or something like  that so in some of these cases the  Saudis have put up substantial sums of  money for these individuals accused of  crimes you know we're talking six-figure  sums of bail money that's put up by  various consulates Saudi consulates here  in the US you know many of the ones  we've looked at here in Oregon because  we've documented a number of cases here  that's come from the closest consulate  to us in Oregon is the Saudi Arabian  consulate Los Angeles so you mentioned  some of some items that were told to you  about how this works from from Saudi  authorities but have they answered any  direct questions about the fact that  some of these individuals are  disappearing and evading justice or have  they not answered the questions related  to that it's been a little difficult  getting detailed explanations from these  Saudi embassy in Washington which has  been sort of our main point of contact  throughout this series but they have  sort of given a blanket denial that they  that their government has helped anybody  or any Saudi citizen break US laws and  escape prosecution in the United States  are there any explanations for any any  parts of this I mean in total it seems  very hard that this would apply but are  there any parts of this that if we said  let's be less cynical skeptical whatever  term you want to apply that there are  some some kind of innocent explanation  for some parts of this well I think  maybe the most  if we could even 